# MASTER RULE: CLARITY

## Purpose
Provide a high-level design principle for building composable, maintainable, and performant systems that complement and extend SOLID principles.

## The CLARITY Principle

> **C L A R I T Y** — *Compose systems so intent is clear, boundaries are pure, performance is architectural, and failure is safe.*

### Core Tenets

#### **C — Compose, don't modify**
- Prefer Decorator/Strategy/Builder patterns over direct modification
- Extend behavior without changing existing code (Open/Closed Principle)
- Use composition over inheritance

#### **L — Layer purity**
- Maintain clean architecture layers: Presentation → Application → Domain → Infrastructure
- Use Adapters at boundaries to prevent leaks
- Domain layer depends only on abstractions, never on infrastructure

#### **A — Architectural performance**
- Achieve speed through structural decisions (caching, indexing, pagination)
- Don't optimize prematurely; optimize architecturally
- Make performance decisions at design time, not implementation time

#### **R — Represent intent**
- Use Parameter Objects and Value Objects to express domain language
- Make implicit concepts explicit through types
- Encode business rules in the type system

#### **I — Input is guarded**
- Validate at boundaries using validation chains and guards
- Fail fast for UX; collect all errors for diagnostics
- Use consistent error codes and messages

#### **T — Telemetry first**
- Add metrics, timeouts, and structured logging everywhere
- Make observability a first-class concern, not an afterthought
- Instrument I/O operations with duration tracking

#### **Y — Yield safe failure**
- Use feature flags for gradual rollouts
- Provide sane defaults for all configurations
- Design for graceful degradation when dependencies fail

---

## Relationship to SOLID

CLARITY extends SOLID by adding:
- **Explicit performance architecture** (not covered by SOLID)
- **Observability as a design principle** (T)
- **Safe failure patterns** (Y)
- **Intent representation through rich types** (R)
- **Boundary validation patterns** (I)

CLARITY assumes SOLID compliance and builds on top of it.

---

## When to Apply

Apply CLARITY when:
- [ ] Designing new systems or major refactors
- [ ] Performance becomes a concern
- [ ] System boundaries need clarification
- [ ] Observability is lacking
- [ ] Error handling is inconsistent
- [ ] Intent is unclear from code

